---
// Page shell for /tools/retirement: intro + the BurnRateLab island (withdrawal
// rates and the historical stress test) + sources and next-tool link.
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import BurnRateLab from "../../components/BurnRateLab.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Will Your Money Last? Retirement Spending"
  description="Turn the 4% rule around: your spending sets the nest egg you need, and guaranteed income shrinks it. Then stress-test the plan against real market history and see sequence-of-returns risk — whether your money survives bad luck, not just an average."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>Will your money last?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Retirement's second problem is living off the pile. Your spending sets the nest
      egg you need — about 25× at a 4% withdrawal rate — and any guaranteed income
      shrinks it. But an average hides the real danger: <em>sequence risk</em>, the
      bad luck of poor early returns. Run your plan through real market history and
      watch the whole spread of outcomes, not just the tidy average.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <BurnRateLab client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>From spending to survival</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Spending sets the target.</strong> Flip the 4% rule around: annual
          spending ÷ withdrawal rate is the nest egg you need (about 25× at 4%). It's
          the fastest way to turn a lifestyle into a number.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Guaranteed income shrinks it.</strong> A pension or Social Security
          check is subtracted from spending first, so only the remaining gap has to
          come from the portfolio. Because of the 25× multiplier, even a modest
          guaranteed income slashes the nest egg you need.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sequence risk is the real test.</strong> Two retirees with the same
          average return can end up worlds apart if one meets a crash early. The
          historical stress test runs your plan through hundreds of real market paths
          so you see the odds it lasts, not just a single hopeful line.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fixed or flexible?</strong> Switch the withdrawal strategy to compare
          two philosophies. <em>Fixed (Bengen)</em> spends the same amount every year and
          leaves you exposed to running out. <em>Guardrails</em> (Guyton-Klinger) flex
          spending — trimming after bad markets, raising after good ones — so the money
          almost never runs dry, but your income varies. The tool estimates your average
          withdrawal rate and shows how much your spending would swing.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This is the drawdown half of retirement. The account decisions that get you
        here — when to claim Social Security, and Roth vs. Traditional — now live in
        the <a href="/personal-finance/retirement-accounts">Retirement Accounts tool</a>.
        Educational only, not financial advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="retirement" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/retirement" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro { padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md); max-width: var(--measure-wide); }
  .lede { font-size: var(--step-1); color: var(--color-text-soft); line-height: 1.5; }
  .wl-explainer { margin-top: var(--space-lg); }
</style>
